# Kaseki Agent API Service - Environment Configuration Example
# 
# Copy this file to .env and update values for your deployment:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # Edit .env with your actual values
#   docker compose up -d
#
# SECURITY: Secrets are stored in host-based files, NOT in .env
# See "REQUIRED: Secret Files" section below for setup instructions

# ===== REQUIRED: Secret Files (NOT in .env) =====

# Kaseki Agent reads secrets from the read-only Docker mount at
# /run/secrets/kaseki/. On a Raspberry Pi, the setup script uses ~/secrets.
# Container UID/GID 10000 needs group-read permission on each secret file.
#
# Required secret files (create these on your host):
#
# 1. LLM Gateway API key (for agent inference)
#    File: ${HOME}/secrets/llm_gateway_api_key
#    Keep the key in this file; do not place it in .env.
#
# 2. Kaseki API Keys (access control for API service)
#    File: ${HOME}/secrets/kaseki_api_keys
#    Content: One API key per line (newline-separated)
#    Setup:
#      printf '%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > "${HOME}/secrets/kaseki_api_keys"
#      chmod 640 "${HOME}/secrets/kaseki_api_keys"
#
# 3. GitHub App Credentials (optional, for PR creation)
#    Files:
#      ${HOME}/secrets/github_app_id           (numeric App ID)
#      ${HOME}/secrets/github_app_client_id    (OAuth Client ID)
#      ${HOME}/secrets/github_app_private_key  (PEM-format private key)
#    Setup:
#      echo "123456" > "${HOME}/secrets/github_app_id"
#      echo "Iv1.abc..." > "${HOME}/secrets/github_app_client_id"
#      cat your-private-key.pem > "${HOME}/secrets/github_app_private_key"
#      chmod 640 "${HOME}/secrets"/github_app_*
#      chgrp 10000 "${HOME}/secrets" "${HOME}/secrets"/*
#
# NOTE: Do NOT store secret values in this .env file. Store them in secret
# files readable by UID/GID 10000 (typically directory mode 750, file mode 640).

# ===== OPTIONAL: Docker Image Configuration =====

# Docker image to use for kaseki-agent containers
# Default: docker.io/cyanautomation/kaseki-agent:latest. scripts/setup-pi.sh
# pulls the image and writes its immutable digest here for both API and workers.
# You can use a local build tag: kaseki-agent:node24-local
KASEKI_API_IMAGE=docker.io/cyanautomation/kaseki-agent:latest
# KASEKI_IMAGE defaults to KASEKI_API_IMAGE so workers use the same revision.

# Host directory mounted read-only at /run/secrets/kaseki.
KASEKI_HOST_SECRETS_DIR=${HOME}/secrets

# Bind only to localhost by default. Set this to the Pi's LAN IP to allow
# other LAN clients to connect (the API key file is still required).
KASEKI_API_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# ===== OPTIONAL: API Service Configuration =====

# API service listen port
# Default: 8080
KASEKI_API_PORT=8080

# API bearer tokens are read from ${HOME}/secrets/kaseki_api_keys, one per line.
# Do not set KASEKI_API_KEYS here; the Pi setup script generates this file.

# Every configured API key has equal access to API endpoints.
# KASEKI_API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=300
# KASEKI_API_DIAGNOSTIC_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=10
# KASEKI_API_WEBHOOK_TESTS_PER_HOUR=10
# KASEKI_API_GITHUB_ISSUES_PER_MINUTE=30

# Exact trusted origins for private or plain-HTTP webhook receivers; default is public HTTPS only.
# KASEKI_WEBHOOK_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://hooks.example.com,http://webhook-receiver:8080

# Log level for kaseki-api service
# Options: debug, info, warn, error
# Default: info
KASEKI_API_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Maximum concurrent kaseki-agent runs
# Adjust based on available system resources
# Default: 1 (recommended for Raspberry Pi 4 memory limits)
KASEKI_API_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=1

# LLM gateway endpoint and model. Provider credential stays in the mounted file.
KASEKI_PROVIDER=gateway
LLM_GATEWAY_URL=https://your-openai-compatible-gateway/v1
LLM_GATEWAY_MODEL=dynamic/kaseki-agent
KASEKI_MODEL=dynamic/kaseki-agent

# ===== OPTIONAL: Agent Execution Configuration =====

# Maximum time (in seconds) for a single Pi agent invocation
# Default: 10800 (180 minutes / 3 hours)
# Increase for larger codebases, decrease to fail fast
KASEKI_AGENT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10800

# Maximum allowed diff size in bytes
# Default: 400000 (400 KB)
# Prevents agents from making too many changes
KASEKI_MAX_DIFF_BYTES=400000

# ===== OPTIONAL: Container User Configuration =====

# Container user and group (UID:GID)
# IMPORTANT: Must match the Dockerfile build (UID 10000)
# Default: 10000:10000
KASEKI_CONTAINER_USER=10000:10000

# ===== OPTIONAL: Docker Socket Group =====

# Docker group ID on the host (for spawning worker containers)
# Get with: stat -c '%g' /var/run/docker.sock
# Default: 985 (common on Linux)
# On macOS/WSL, use the actual group ID from the socket
DOCKER_GID=985

# ===== OPTIONAL: User Configuration =====

# Container API user (if different from default)
# Default: 10000:10000
KASEKI_API_USER=10000:10000

# ===== OPTIONAL: Secret File Locations (Advanced) =====

# If your secret files are in non-standard locations, override these:
# (Most users should not need to change these)

# KASEKI_API_KEYS_FILE: Path to API keys file
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY_FILE: Path to OpenRouter API key file
# GITHUB_APP_ID_FILE: Path to GitHub App ID file
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID_FILE: Path to GitHub App Client ID file
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE: Path to GitHub App Private Key file
#
# Compose mounts the host directory at /run/secrets/kaseki; use canonical
# filenames there rather than overriding container-side file paths.

# ===== OPTIONAL: GitHub App Authentication =====

# If using GitHub App for authenticated git operations (optional)
# Create the corresponding secret files (see "REQUIRED: Secret Files" above)
# No configuration needed here - the system will detect them automatically

# ===== SYSTEM DIRECTORIES =====

# These are usually set automatically, but can be overridden:

# Results directory (persists run artifacts)
# Default: /agents/kaseki-results
# KASEKI_RESULTS_DIR=/agents/kaseki-results

# API log directory
# Default: /var/log/kaseki-api
# KASEKI_API_LOG_DIR=/var/log/kaseki-api

# ===== DEPLOYMENT NOTES =====

# 1. Raspberry Pi setup: bash scripts/setup-pi.sh
#    This creates /agents with UID/GID 10000 ownership and avoids broad
#    permissions such as 777.

# 2. Set up secret files (see "REQUIRED: Secret Files" section above):
#    mkdir -p "${HOME}/secrets"
#    chmod 750 "${HOME}/secrets"
#    # Write secrets to files and set owner/group so UID/GID 10000 can read.
#    chgrp 10000 "${HOME}/secrets" "${HOME}/secrets"/*
#    chmod 640 "${HOME}/secrets"/*

# 3. Ensure Docker socket is accessible:
#    export DOCKER_GID="$(stat -c '%g' /var/run/docker.sock)"

# 4. For production, use a reverse proxy (nginx) for HTTPS:
#    Listen on 443 (HTTPS) and proxy to localhost:8080

# 5. Set up log rotation if logging to files:
#    sudo cp ops/logrotate/kaseki-api /etc/logrotate.d/

# 6. Monitor API health:
#    curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $(head -n 1 "${HOME}/secrets/kaseki_api_keys")" http://127.0.0.1:8080/health
